---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
---

<Layout
  title="Lumen"
  description="Lumen is an open source, offline-first, privacy-by-design assistant for people with a limitation."
>
  <div class="hero">
    <div class="hero-glow" aria-hidden="true"></div>
    <h1 class="hero-enter" style="animation-delay: 60ms">A little light, when it's needed.</h1>
    <p class="lead hero-enter" style="animation-delay: 220ms">
      Lumen turns a smartphone into help for people with a limitation in seeing, hearing, speaking,
      reading, remembering, moving, or staying safe. Everything runs on the phone, no internet
      required.
    </p>
    <div class="cta-row hero-enter" style="animation-delay: 380ms">
      <a class="button primary" href="/modules/">Discover the modules</a>
      <a class="button secondary" href="/demo/">Try the demo</a>
      <a class="button secondary" href="/screenshots/">See screenshots</a>
      <a class="button secondary" href="https://github.com/lumen-assistive">View on GitHub</a>
    </div>
  </div>

  <section>
    <h2>One engine, eight modules</h2>
    <p>
      Lumen is built for the <strong>GF Marilli Prize</strong> by HackersGen. A single shared
      multimodal engine, the "senses engine" (vision, audio, text), powers eight independent
      modules, delivered as plugins. A contributor can write a new module without touching the
      engine, following the same interfaces the official modules use.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Adapts to who uses it</h2>
    <p>
      On first launch, five simple questions turn on only the modules a person needs, and decide how
      the interface should behave: voice and large buttons for low vision, a fully visual interface
      with vibration for deaf users, a simplified layout for elderly users, a complete layout for
      students.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Stays on the phone</h2>
    <p>
      Every essential feature works with no network connection. No audio or image ever leaves the
      device unless the person explicitly chooses to share it. An optional server lets caregivers
      sync, only if turned on voluntarily, and whatever it receives is encrypted before it ever
      leaves the phone.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Project status</h2>
    <p>
      Lumen is in active, open source development. The senses engine (<a
        href="https://github.com/lumen-assistive/lumen-core">lumen-core</a
      >), the plugin SDK (<a href="https://github.com/lumen-assistive/lumen-sdk">lumen-sdk</a>), and
      an early build of the app (<a href="https://github.com/lumen-assistive/lumen-app"
        >lumen-app</a
      >) are already published. See <a href="/modules/">Modules</a> for the status of each one.
    </p>
    <p>
      We do not publish user data, metrics, or partnerships until they are real and verifiable.
      Results from real user testing and any collaborations will be published here once available.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="wide">
    <h2>Frequently asked</h2>
    <div class="faq-list">
      <details class="faq-item">
        <summary>Is there an app I can install?</summary>
        <p>
          Not yet. <code>lumen-app</code> exists and runs, but there is no signed Android release. See
          <a href="/download/">Download</a>.
        </p>
      </details>
      <details class="faq-item">
        <summary>Does anything I say or that Lumen sees leave my phone?</summary>
        <p>
          No, not by default. Every essential feature runs on-device. The only exceptions are
          features you explicitly turn on and consent to, like sharing status with a caregiver or
          syncing with the optional server, and in those cases what is sent is end-to-end
          encrypted. See <a href="/docs/privacy/data-handling/">Data handling in detail</a>.
        </p>
      </details>
      <details class="faq-item">
        <summary>Why don't the modules use the camera, microphone, or accelerometer yet?</summary>
        <p>
          Because <code>lumen-core</code> has no native sensor bridges yet. Every module's real,
          tested business logic (parsing, detection algorithms, state machines) is implemented and
          ready; it is simply not connected to live hardware yet. See
          <a href="/docs/introduction/architecture/">Architecture overview</a>.
        </p>
      </details>
      <details class="faq-item">
        <summary>What license is the code under?</summary>
        <p>
          Apache License 2.0, in every repository. Third-party models and datasets are licensed
          separately and tracked in
          <a href="https://github.com/lumen-assistive/lumen-models">lumen-models</a>. See
          <a href="/docs/privacy/licensing/">Model and dataset licensing</a>.
        </p>
      </details>
    </div>
    <p><a href="/docs/reference/faq/">See the full FAQ</a></p>
  </section>
</Layout>
